  narrativeContent: `Before there were roads across Canada there were rivers, and before the fur traders paddled them, the people who lived along them had used them for thousands of years. The Anishinaabe knew every portage between Lake Superior and Lake Winnipeg. The Cree travelled the Saskatchewan from the forest to the plains. The Dene crossed Methye Portage to the Athabasca. The Dakelh and Sekani knew the rivers over the Rockies. When Europeans came, they followed these people, and usually they were guided by them.

## From the Great Lakes to the west

The water road west began at Georgian Bay, where Champlain and Brûlé came in 1615 and the Jesuits built Sainte-Marie among the Wendat in 1639. From Lake Superior the Grand Portage carried the canoes around the falls of the Pigeon River, and then the route ran through Rainy Lake, down the Rainy River to Lake of the Woods, and down the falls of the Winnipeg River to Lake Winnipeg. Jacques de Noyon went this way in 1688 and La Vérendrye built his forts along it in the 1730s. For a hundred years every canoe from Montreal to the west came this way.

## The Saskatchewan

From Lake Winnipeg the Saskatchewan led west across the plains. It divides into two branches: the North Saskatchewan, which rises in the Rockies and flows past Edmonton, and the South Saskatchewan, made by the Bow and the Oldman from the Blackfoot country. Anthony Henday travelled with the Cree along the North Saskatchewan in 1754 and saw the Rockies. From the 1770s the traders built posts up the river, each company a few hundred steps from the other: Hudson House, Buckingham House and Fort George, Edmonton House and Fort Augustus, Rocky Mountain House. The posts traded furs, but what they needed most was pemmican, the dried buffalo meat that the Cree and Métis made on the plains and that fed the canoe brigades going north.

## Over the height of land

From the Saskatchewan at Cumberland House a small rapid river, the Sturgeon-Weir, led to Frog Portage and the Churchill River, and from the Churchill a twenty-kilometre portage, the Methye Portage, led over the height of land to the Clearwater River and the Athabasca country. Peter Pond crossed it in 1778. From the Athabasca the Slave River ran north, past rapids that had to be portaged for sixteen miles, to Great Slave Lake and the Mackenzie. The Liard came in from the west, and up it Robert Campbell found the way to the Yukon.

## Over the mountains

Alexander Mackenzie went up the Peace in 1793 to Finlay Forks, where a Sekani elder told him to take the Parsnip. He did, crossed the divide to the Fraser, and came down the Bella Coola River with Nuxalk guides to the sea. Simon Fraser followed him up the Parsnip to build Fort McLeod, and then up the Nechako to Stuart Lake, where Fort St. James stood beside Kwah's village for the next hundred years. Peter Fidler, David Thompson and James Hector went into the mountains from the Bow.

## The rivers today

The water road is still there. Fort Edmonton and Fort Carlton, Fort Smith and Fort St. James stand on it. Treaty 6 was signed at Fort Carlton on the North Saskatchewan and Treaty 7 at Blackfoot Crossing on the Bow. The names along it are Cree, Blackfoot, Dene and Anishinaabe: Saskatchewan, Manitoba, Qu'Appelle, Winnipeg. When you follow these rivers on the map, you are following the oldest roads in the country.`,
